\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Gluing two Gaussian laws across a separator}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{V=V_1\cup V_2}, #{S=V_1\cap V_2},
#{A=V_1\setminus S} and #{B=V_2\setminus S}. Suppose positive-definite
centered Gaussian laws #{\mu_1,\mu_2} have the same marginal
#{\mu_S}. Write #{p_1,p_2,p_S} for their positive Lebesgue densities.
There is exactly one positive Gaussian extension with
#{\phi_A\mathrel{\perp\mkern-9mu\perp}\phi_B\mid\phi_S}. Its density is
##{p(a,s,b)=\frac{p_1(a,s)p_2(s,b)}{p_S(s)}.}
If #{S=\varnothing}, take #{p_S=1}; this is the product law.}

\p{Let #{\Lambda_1,\Lambda_2,\Lambda_S} denote the three marginal
precision operators. For #{T\subseteq V}, let #{i_T:\mathbb R^T\to\mathbb R^V}
be the counting inclusion, extending functions by zero. Padding is
##{\operatorname{pad}_T(\Lambda)=i_T\Lambda i_T^*.}
The extension's precision is
##{Q=\operatorname{pad}_{V_1}(\Lambda_1)
     +\operatorname{pad}_{V_2}(\Lambda_2)
     -\operatorname{pad}_S(\Lambda_S).}
In particular, if a positive global Gaussian law has #{S} separating
#{A} and #{B} in its precision graph, this formula reconstructs its
precision from the indicated marginals. The operators #{\Lambda_i}
are marginal precisions, not the compressions of #{Q} to those
vertex sets. Their counting matrices are given in \ref{fdip-00LV}.}

\proof{
\p{Order the coordinates as #{A}, #{S}, #{B} and use the counting
basis. Factor the proposed density as
#{p_S(s)p_1(a\mid s)p_2(b\mid s)}. Successively integrating over
#{a,b,s} gives total mass one. Integrating just #{b} or just #{a}
recovers #{p_1} or #{p_2}, respectively; its conditional density is
visibly a product. Completing the two local squares writes the quadratic
form in its exponent #{-q/2} as
##{\begin{aligned}
q(a,s,b)={}&s^T\Lambda_Ss
 +(a-M_1s)^TD_1(a-M_1s)\\
 &+(b-M_2s)^TD_2(b-M_2s),
\end{aligned}}
where #{D_1,D_2>0} are the conditional precisions and #{M_1,M_2}
are the conditional mean maps. This form is positive definite:
if it is zero, its three nonnegative terms force first #{s=0},
then #{a=b=0}. The density is therefore a centered positive Gaussian.
Subtracting the three density exponents gives the displayed padding
formula. These statements include empty coordinate spaces.}

\p{For any extension with the asserted conditional independence,
its conditional marginals must equal the two prescribed conditional
marginals almost everywhere. Its density is consequently the same
product almost everywhere, proving uniqueness as a probability law.
For an existing global law the [Markov theorem](fdip-0065) supplies
this independence. This is the density factorization underlying
\citet{Propositions 1–2, pp. 139–141}{speed1986gaussian}; the
construction here also proves positive-definite existence from the
two compatible input laws.}
}

\p{The [four-cycle calculation](fdip-006B) displays the marginal
precisions and reconstructs every entry by this formula.}
